# 9 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.
Japan | definition | Japan is an island country in East Asia made up of more than 6,000 islands, the largest of which are Honshu, Hokkaido, Kyushu and Shoku. Its capital is Tokyo, its currency is the yen, and its population is roughly 125 million. Shinto and Buddhism have both shaped the country for centuries, and Mount Fuji at 3,776 metres is the highest point. | archipelago, tokyo, yen, fuji, east asia | how many islands does japan have, is tokyo the capital of japan | Hapon | Hapon
Brazil | definition | Brazil is the largest country in South America and the fifth largest in the world by area, covering most of the eastern part of the continent. Its capital is Brasilia, which was built from scratch in the 1960s to replace Rio de Janeiro as the seat of government. The Amazon rainforest covers roughly two thirds of the country, Portuguese is the official language, and the real is the currency. | brasilia, amazon, portuguese, real, rainforest, south america | what is the capital of brazil, why is brasilia not the biggest city in brazil | Brasil | Brasil
Egypt | definition | Egypt is a country in north-east Africa, almost entirely desert, and about ninety-five percent of its people live along the Nile. Cairo is both the capital and the largest city. The ancient pyramids at Giza were built as tombs for pharaohs of the Old Kingdom, and the Suez Canal links the Mediterranean to the Red Sea, which has made Egypt a crossroads of world trade for two thousand years. | cairo, nile, pyramid, giza, suez, pharaoh | what is egypt famous for, is the nile in egypt, where is the suez canal | Ehipto | Ehipto
Kenya | definition | Kenya is a country in East Africa whose capital is Nairobi. Swahili and English are the official languages, and Kenya is one of the few countries where you can drive across two climate zones in a single day. The Great Rift Valley runs through it, and the annual wildebeest migration between the Serengeti in Tanzania and the Maasai Mara in Kenya is one of the largest animal movements on the planet. | nairobi, swahili, rift valley, serengeti, maasai mara, wildebeest | what is the great rift valley, where is the wildebeest migration | Kenya | Kenya
Canada | definition | Canada is the second largest country in the world by total area, and the largest that has only two coastlines. Its capital is Ottawa, which surprises people who assume it is Toronto or Montreal. English and French are both official, the maple leaf is the national symbol, and the country spans six time zones from the Pacific to the Atlantic. Most of the population lives within a few hundred kilometres of the southern border. | ottawa, maple leaf, six time zones, north america, bilingual | why is ottawa the capital of canada, what languages are spoken in canada | Kanada | Kanada
Australia | definition | Australia is both a country and the smallest continent, and it is the only nation whose entire territory lies within a single continent. The capital is Canberra, chosen as a compromise between Sydney and Melbourne rather than either of them. It is home to most of the world's marsupials, including kangaroos, koalas and wombats, and the Great Barrier Reef off Queensland is the largest structure built by living organisms anywhere on Earth. | canberra, kangaroo, koala, great barrier reef, marsupial | why is canberra the capital of australia, what animals live only in australia | Australya | Australya
Norway | definition | Norway is a country in Scandinavia whose capital is Oslo. Its coastline is defined by deep fjords, narrow sea-filled valleys cut by glaciers, which is why it is sometimes said to have the longest coastline in Europe relative to its size. The aurora borealis is visible in the far north during the dark winter months, and offshore petroleum has made it one of the wealthiest countries per person in the world. | oslo, fjord, aurora borealis, northern lights, scandinavia | what is a fjord, where can you see the northern lights | Norwega | Norwega
Iceland | definition | Iceland is a country in the North Atlantic built on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, where tectonic plates pull apart and the crust is continuously replaced. Reykjavik is the capital and the largest city. It has almost no mosquitoes, largely because of the cool, windy, wet climate, and its hot springs and geysers are heated by magma rather than by fuel. | reykjavik, volcano, geyser, geothermal, mid-atlantic ridge | why does iceland have no mosquitoes, are there volcanoes in iceland | Iceland | Iceland
Peru | definition | Peru is a country on the western coast of South America whose capital is Lima. It is three-quarters of the way down the Andes, so it has a stretch of Amazon rainforest in the east and a narrow desert coast in the west. Machu Picchu, the Inca citadel in the cloud forest, was built in the fifteenth century and is still standing. The Nazca Lines in the south were made by removing dark stones to expose lighter ground beneath. | lima, machu picchu, inca, andes, nazca lines | what is machu picchu, who built machu picchu | Peru | Peru
